king the logs for each service might reveal if any services are having issues. Alternatively, using get_services from Grafana could list the services, but since the user already has the service names from the deployments, maybe checking logs is more direct. Another option is to use get_dependency_graph to see if there are any dependencies missing or failing. But since the initial metrics query failed, maybe the services aren\'t exposing the necessary metrics. Alternatively, checking the service endpoints with kubectl get endpoints could show if services are reachable. But the user might need to check logs first for any immediate errors. So, the next step is to check the logs of the services that are part of the hotel reservation system. Let\'s pick a few key services like frontend, reservation, and mongodb-reservation to check their logs for any errors.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: Since all deployments are healthy but the PromQL query failed, the issue might be with metrics exposure or service misconfiguration. Checking logs for key services (e.g., `reservation`, `mongodb-reservation`, `frontend`) will reveal runtime errors, failed requests, or misconfigurations that could explain missing metrics or service instability.  \n\ntool_call: {  \n  "command": "kubectl logs -n hotel-reservation reservation-0"  \n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=406, prompt_tokens=2417, total_tokens=2823, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe3d-8188-7a20-a1ad-ab0c490509f3-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2417, 'output_tokens': 406, 'total_tokens': 2823}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', addition