etrics on the expected port or path. \n\nWait, the reservation service\'s endpoints are on port 8087. But maybe the metrics are exposed on a different port, like 9090 or another common port for exporters. For example, many Prometheus exporters use port 9100 or 9101. If the reservation service is using an exporter, it might be on a different port than the application\'s main port.\n\nSo the next step should be to check the service configuration for the reservation service. Specifically, looking at the Service YAML to see which port is exposed and what the target port is. If the service is set up to expose port 8087, but the exporter is on a different port, like 9100, then Prometheus wouldn\'t be scraping the metrics correctly.\n\nTherefore, using kubectl to describe the reservation service would show the port mappings. That way, they can verify if the metrics are being exposed on the correct port. If the service is using the wrong port, they can adjust it to point to the correct metrics port. Alternatively, if the service isn\'t configured to expose metrics at all, that\'s another issue.\n\nSo the tool to use here is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl describe service reservation -n hotel-reservation". This will provide details about the service, including the ports and target ports, helping to identify if the metrics are being exposed correctly.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The absence of `http_requests_total` metrics indicates that the `reservation` service may not be exposing metrics on the expected port (e.g., 8087) or the Prometheus exporter is misconfigured. Checking the service\'s YAML configuration will confirm the exposed port and target port, ensuring metrics are accessible via the correct endpoint.  \n\ntool_call: {  \n  "command": "kubectl describe service reservation -n hotel-reservation"  \n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 520, 'prompt_tokens': 7415, 'total_tokens': 7935, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe3e-5d40-7ca2-9ca8-d8e25056002a-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7415, 'output_tokens': 520, 'total_tokens': 7935}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='4dacded5-ac0f-45b2-896f-6a83db2f902e'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the hotel reservation application. They've already checked the deployments and pods, and everything seems to be running. The problem seems to be with the metrics not being collected properly. The initial PromQL query for HTTP requests failed, and when they checked the endpoints, the reservation service had an endpoint on port 8087. But when they tried to query the http_requests_total metric for the blackbox-hotel-reservation job, there were no results.\n\nSo, the next step is to check the service configuration for the reservation service. By describing the service, they can see the target port and the port the service is exposing. If the service is set up correctly, the metrics should be accessible on the specified port. If not, maybe the exporter isn't running on the correct port, or the service isn't properly configured to expose the metrics. This would help determine if the issue is with the service configuration or the exporter itself. Let me call the kubectl describe command to get more details.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe service reservation -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-beeae98b3e3db1ff', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 251, 'prompt_tokens': 8423, 'total_tokens': 8674, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe3e-7684-7060-8054-a154cdda9e61-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe service reservation -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-beeae98b3e3db1ff', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 8423, 'output_tokens': 251, 'total_tokens': 8674}), ToolMessage(content='Name:                     reservation\nNamespace:                hotel-reservation\nLabels:                   io.kompose.service=reservation\nAnnotations:              kompose.cmd: kompose convert\n                          kompose.version: 1.22.0 (955b78124)\nSelector:                 io.kompose.service=reservation\nType:                     ClusterIP\nIP Family Policy:         SingleStack\nIP Families:              IPv4\nIP:                       10.96.193.45\nIPs:                      10.96.193.45\nPort:                     8087  8087/TCP\nTargetPort:               8087/TCP\nEndpoints:                <none>\nSession Affinity:         None\nInternal Traffic Policy:  Cluster\nEvents:                   <none>\n', id='e3db90bf-fac2-4c44-a619-7d6e8326500a', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-beeae98b3e3db1ff'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='2dfef667-bc1a-4c10-9099-f942c9baa82b'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the hotel reservation application. So far, they\'ve checked the deployments, pods, services, and metrics. The key issue seems to be that the `http_requests_total` metric isn\'t showing up in Prometheus, which is crucial for monitoring HTTP requests.\n\nFirst, they checked the endpoints for the `reservation` service and found that the ENDPOINTS field is empty. That means no pods are actually assigned to this service. But earlier, when they checked the pods, all deployments were healthy. Wait, maybe there\'s a mismatch here. The `reservation` deployment might be running, but the service isn\'t correctly pointing to the pods. \n\nLooking at the service description for `reservation`, the ENDPOINTS are listed as <none>, which indicates that the service isn\'t able to find any pods. But the deployment for `reservation` was shown as having 1/1 pods ready. That\'s confusing. Maybe the service is using the wrong label selector? The service\'s selector is `io.kompose.service=reservation`, but the deployment\'s labels might not match that. \n\nThe user should check the deployment\'s YAML to see what labels are applied. If the deployment\'s pods don\'t have the label `io.kompose.service=reservation`, the service won\'t be able to find them, leading to empty endpoints. That would explain why the metrics aren\'t being scraped—because the service isn\'t actually pointing to any running pods. \n\nSo the next step is to describe the `reservation` deployment to check its labels and ensure they match the service\'s selector. If there\'s a mismatch, that\'s the root cause. Also, verifying the port configurations to make sure the metrics endpoint is correctly exposed. If the service is using the wrong port or the pods aren\'t exposing metrics on that port, Prometheus won\'t scrape them. \n\nAnother angle: maybe the `reservation` service isn\'t configured to expose metrics. If the application isn\'t set up with an HTTP server that exposes metrics on port 8087, then Prometheus can\'t collect them. But the user already checked the endpoints and found none, so the service isn\'t even reaching the pods. \n\nSo the main issues could be either a label selector mismatch between the service and deployment, or the deployment not being properly configured to run the application with the necessary metrics endpoints. Checking the deployment\'s YAML and the service\'s selector should clarify this. If the labels don\'t match, that\'s the root cause. If they do, then maybe the application isn\'t exposing metrics correctly, but that\'s a different issue.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The `reservation` service has no endpoints, meaning no pods are assigned to it. This suggests a mismatch between the service\'s selector (`io.kompose.service=reservation`) and the actual labels on the `reservation` deployment pods. Checking the deployment\'s YAML will confirm if the labels match and ensure the service can discover pods.  \n\ntool_call: {  \n  "command": "kubectl describe deployment reservation -n hotel-reservation"  \n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 638, 'prompt_tokens': 7788, 'total_tokens': 8426, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe3e-867d-7662-abe5-52c192159c5d-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7788, 'output_tokens': 638, 'total_tokens': 8426}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='091bcc53-9081-4e0f-b2e2-a9658159477e'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the Hotel Reservation application. So far, they've checked the deployments, services, and endpoints. The key issue seems to be with the 'reservation' service not having any endpoints, which means no pods are attached to it.\n\nFirst, they used 'kubectl get deployments' and saw that the 'reservation' deployment is healthy with 1/1 replicas. Then, when checking the service 'reservation', the endpoints were empty. That's a problem because if there are no endpoints, the service can't route traffic to any pods.\n\nThe next step was to describe the service 'reservation', which showed the selector is 'io.kompose.service=reservation'. But the endpoints are <none>, so the service isn't finding any pods. The logical next move is to check the deployment's configuration to see if the labels match the service's selector. \n\nSo, the user should run 'kubectl describe deployment reservation -n hotel-reservation' to look at the deployment's details. This will show the pod template's labels. If the deployment's pods have a different label, like maybe 'app: reservation' instead of 'io.kompose.service=reservation', that would explain why the service isn't finding any endpoints. \n\nBy comparing the service's selector with the deployment's pod labels, they can identify a mismatch. Once they find that, they can correct the labels in either the service or the deployment to ensure they match, allowing the service to properly route traffic to the pods. That should resolve the issue with the endpoints being empty.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe deployment reservation -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9a01bd5fe02132a7', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 359, 'prompt_tokens': 8795, 'total_tokens': 9154, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe3e-a54b-70a1-99ae-2c521cb2f642-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe deployment re