isn\'t showing up in Prometheus, which is crucial for monitoring HTTP requests.\n\nFirst, they checked the endpoints for the `reservation` service and found that the ENDPOINTS field is empty. That means no pods are actually assigned to this service. But earlier, when they checked the pods, all deployments were healthy. Wait, maybe there\'s a mismatch here. The `reservation` deployment might be running, but the service isn\'t correctly pointing to the pods. \n\nLooking at the service description for `reservation`, the ENDPOINTS are listed as <none>, which indicates that the service isn\'t able to find any pods. But the deployment for `reservation` was shown as having 1/1 pods ready. That\'s confusing. Maybe the service is using the wrong label selector? The service\'s selector is `io.kompose.service=reservation`, but the deployment\'s labels might not match that. \n\nThe user should check the deployment\'s YAML to see what labels are applied. If the deployment\'s pods don\'t have the label `io.kompose.service=reservation`, the service won\'t be able to find them, leading to empty endpoints. That would explain why the metrics aren\'t being scraped—because the service isn\'t actually pointing to any running pods. \n\nSo the next step is to describe the `reservation` deployment to check its labels and ensure they match the service\'s selector. If there\'s a mismatch, that\'s the root cause. Also, verifying the port configurations to make sure the metrics endpoint is correctly exposed. If the service is using the wrong port or the pods aren\'t exposing metrics on that port, Prometheus won\'t scrape them. \n\nAnother angle: maybe the `reservation` service isn\'t configured to expose metrics. If the application isn\'t set up with an HTTP server that exposes metrics on port 8087, then Prometheus can\'t collect them. But the user already checked the endpoints and found none, so the service isn\'t even reaching the pods. \n\nSo the main issues could be either a label selector mismatch between the service and deployment, or the deployment not being properly configured to run the application with the necessary metrics endpoints. Checking the deployment\'s YAML and the service\'s selector should clarify this. If the labels don\'t match, that\'s the root cause. If they do, then maybe the application isn\'t exposing metrics correctly, but that\'s a different issue.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The `reservation` service has no endpoints, meaning no pods are assigned to it. This suggests a mismatch between the service\'s selector (`io.kompose.service=reservation`) and the actual labels on the `reservation` deployment pods. Checking the deployment\'s YAML will confirm if the labels match and ensure the service can discover pods.  \n\ntool_call: {  \n  "command": "kubectl describe deployment reservation -n hotel-reservation"  \n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 638, 'prompt_tokens': 7788, 'total_tokens': 8426, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe3e-867d-7662-abe5-52c192159c5d-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7788, 'output_tokens': 638, 'total_tokens': 8426}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='091bcc53-9081-4e0f-b2e2-a9658159477e'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the Hotel Reservation application. So far, they've checked the deployments, services, and endpoints. The key issue seems to be with the 'reservation' service not having any endpoints, which means no pods are attached to it.\n\nFirst, they used 'kubectl get deployments' and saw that the 'reservation' deployment is healthy with 1/1 replicas. Then, when checking the service 'reservation', the endpoints were empty. That's a problem because if there are no endpoints, the service can't route traffic to any pods.\n\nThe next step was to describe the service 'reservation', which showed the selector is 'io.kompose.service=reservation'. But the endpoints are <none>, so the service isn't finding any pods. The logical next move is to check the deployment's configuration to see if the labels match the service's selector. \n\nSo, the user should run 'kubectl describe deployment reservation -n hotel-reservation' to look at the deployment's details. This will show the pod template's labels. If the deployment's pods have a different label, like maybe 'app: reservation' instead of 'io.kompose.service=reservation', that would explain why the service isn't finding any endpoints. \n\nBy comparing the service's selector with the deployment's pod labels, they can identify a mismatch. Once they find that, they can correct the labels in either the service or the deployment to ensure they match, allowing the service to properly route traffic to the pods. That should resolve the issue with the endpoints being empty.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe deployment reservation -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9a01bd5fe02132a7', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 359, 'prompt_tokens': 8795, 'total_tokens': 9154, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe3e-a54b-70a1-99ae-2c521cb2f642-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe deployment reservation -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9a01bd5fe02132a7', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 8795, 'output_tokens': 359, 'total_tokens': 9154}), ToolMessage(content='Name:                   reservation\nNamespace:              hotel-reservation\nCreationTimestamp:      Tue, 17 Mar 2026 23:57:07 +0000\nLabels:                 io.kompose.service=reservation\nAnnotations:            deployment.kubernetes.io/revision: 1\n                        kompose.cmd: kompose convert\n                        kompose.version: 1.22.0 (955b78124)\nSelector:               io.kompose.service=reservation\nReplicas:               1 desired | 1 updated | 1 total | 1 available | 0 unavailable\nStrategyType:           RollingUpdate\nMinReadySeconds:        0\nRollingUpdateStrategy:  25% max unavailable, 25% max surge\nPod Template:\n  Labels:       io.kompose.service=reservation\n  Annotations:  kompose.cmd: kompose convert\n                kompose.version: 1.22.0 (955b78124)\n                sidecar.istio.io/statsInclusionPrefixes:\n                  cluster.outbound,cluster_manager,listener_manager,http_mixer_filter,tcp_mixer_filter,server,cluster.xds-grp,listener,connection_manager\n                sidecar.istio.io/statsInclusionRegexps: http.*\n  Containers:\n   hotel-reserv-reservation:\n    Image:      yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest\n    Port:       8087/TCP\n    Host Port:  0/TCP\n    Command:\n      reservation\n    Limits:\n      cpu:  1\n    Requests:\n      cpu:  100m\n    Environment:\n      JAEGER_SAMPLE_RATIO:  1\n    Mounts:                 <none>\n  Volumes:                  <none>\n  Node-Selectors:           <none>\n  Tolerations:              <none>\nConditions:\n  Type           Status  Reason\n  ----           ------  ------\n  Progressing    True    NewReplicaSetAvailable\n  Available      True    MinimumReplicasAvailable\nOldReplicaSets:  <none>\nNewReplicaSet:   reservation-7bb77b596b (1/1 replicas created)\nEvents:\n  Type    Reason             Age    From                   Message\n  ----    ------             ----   ----                   -------\n  Normal  ScalingReplicaSet  3m41s  deployment-controller  Scaled up replica set reservation-7bb77b596b to 1\n', id='e7cc71ff-b584-437f-80a1-7e980cb34289', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9a01bd5fe02132a7'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='627dcc8d-2ee9-4f37-b382-57c76e12bf28'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose issues with the hotel reservation application. So far, they\'ve checked the deployments, services, endpoints, and metrics. The key findings are that the `reservation` service has no endpoints, meaning no pods are assigned to it. The service\'s selector is `io.kompose.service=reservation`, and the deployment for `reservation` also uses the same selector. But the endpoints are empty.\n\nWait, the deployment\'s pods should have the same label as the service\'s selector. Let me check if the pods actually exist. The user previously ran `kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation` and saw that `reservation-0` was running. But the service\'s endpoints are <none>. That\'s conflicting. Maybe the pod isn\'t labeled correctly? Or maybe there\'s a typo in the labels?\n\nThe deployment\'s pod template has the label `io.kompose.service=reservation`, which matches the service\'s selector. But if the endpoints are empty, that suggests that the service isn\'t able to find any pods with that label. Could there be a problem with the pod\'s labels? Let me check the pod\'s details. The user should run `kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation -o jsonpath=\'{.items[*].metadata.labels}\'` to see the labels on the reservation pod. If the label is missing or different, that\'s the issue. Alternatively, maybe the pod isn\'t running, but the previous command showed it as running. Hmm.\n\nWait, the user ran `kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation` and saw `reservation-0` with status 1/1. So the pod is running. But the service\'s endpoints are still empty. That\'s strange. Maybe the service is misconfigured. Let me check the service\'s YAML again. The service `reservation` has a port 8087, target port 8087, but no endpoints. The selector is correct, so why no endpoints? Maybe the pod isn\'t exposing the correct port for metrics? Or maybe the service is not correctly referencing the pod\'s IP. Alternatively, maybe the pod isn\'t ready yet, but the deployment shows it\'s available.\n\nWait, the deployment\'s status shows 1 available replica. So the pod should be ready. But the service isn\'t seeing it. Maybe there\'s a network policy blocking the service from accessing the pod? Or maybe the pod\'s IP isn\'t being assigned correctly. Alternatively, maybe the service is in the wrong namespace? No, the namespace is correct. \n\nAnother angle: the user checked metrics for `http_requests_total` but got no results. The service might not be exposing metrics on the expected port. The `reservation` service\'s port is 8087, but if the application isn\'t serving metrics on that port, Prometheus can\'t scrape them. But the user\'s main issue is the missing endpoints. So the root cause is likely that the service isn\'t correctly linked to the pod, possibly due to a label mismatch or a misconfigured service. \n\nWait, the user already checked the deployment and service labels. They match. So why no endpoints? Maybe the pod isn\'t actually running, but the `kubectl get pods` shows it as running. Let me check the pod\'s status in more detail. The user should run `kubectl describe pod reservation-0 -n hotel-reservation` to see if there are any issues with the pod, like crash loops or readiness probes failing. If the pod is crashing, the service wouldn\'t have endpoints. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is using the wrong port. The service\'s port is 8087, but the pod\'s container might be listening on a different port. Wait, the deployment\'s container has port 8087/TCP, so that should be correct. \n\nWait, the user also checked the endpoints for the service and found none. So the service isn\'t able to find any pods with the selector. This could be due to a label mismatch, but the labels seem to match. Maybe the pod wasn\'t properly created? Or there\'s a typo in the labels. Let me check the pod\'s labels again. \n\nThe user should run `kubectl get pod reservation-0 -n hotel-reservation -o jsonpath=\'{.metadata.labels}\'` to confirm the labels. If the label is missing or different, that\'s the problem. If the labels are correct, then maybe the service is not properly configured. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is not in the same namespace as the pods. But the namespace is `hotel-reservation` for both. \n\nAnother possibility: the service is using a different selector. Let me check the service\'s YAML again. The service\'s selector is `io.kompose.service=reservation`, which matches the deployment\'s pod label. So that\'s correct. \n\nHmm, this is confusing. The pod exists, the labels match, but the service has no endpoints. Maybe there\'s a bug in the Kubernetes setup, or maybe the pod isn\'t actually running. Wait, the user ran `kubectl get pods` and saw `reservation-0` as running. But maybe the pod is in a CrashLoopBackOff state? Let me check the pod\'s status with `kubectl describe pod reservation-0 -n hotel-reservation`. If there are errors in the pod\'s logs, that could explain why the service isn\'t seeing it. \n\nThe user previously tried to get logs for `reservation-0` but maybe the logs aren\'t showing any issues. Alternatively, maybe the pod isn\'t exposing the correct metrics port. If the application isn\'t serving metrics on port 8087, then Prometheus can\'t scrape them, leading to the missing metrics. But the main issue here is the missing endpoints, which is a separate problem. \n\nWait, the user\'s main goal is to find the root cause of any faults. The initial problem might be that the `reservation` service isn\'t functioning because it has no endpoints, which could be due to the pod not being properly assigned. So the next step is to check the pod\'s details to see why it\'s not being discovered by the service. \n\nTherefore, the user should run `kubectl describe pod reservation-0 -n hotel-reservation` to check for any issues with the pod, such as readiness probes failing, or the container not starting correctly. If the pod is crashing, that\'s the root cause. Alternatively, if the pod is running but not labeled correctly, that\'s the issue. \n\nSo the next tool call should be to describe the pod to get more details on its status and any potential errors.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The `reservation` service has no endpoints despite the pod being listed as running. This suggests a mismatch between the pod\'s labels and the service\'s selector, or the pod is not properly assigned. Describing the pod will reveal its status, readiness, and any errors preventing it from being discovered by the service.  \n\ntool_call: {  \n  "command": "kubectl describe pod reservation-0 -n hotel-reservation"  \n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1460, 'prompt_tokens': 8516, 'total_tokens': 9976, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe3e-ba77-7d12-9696-c4586bb7e010-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 8516, 'output_tokens': 1460, 'total_tokens': 9976}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='4177642f-d29e-46af-bfc2-558dab6bfadc'), AIMessa