 justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b47f3a9c-1c93-41ba-937c-f732cd249d96'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s try to figure out why the reservation service isn\'t showing up in the endpoints. The user mentioned that the service has no endpoints, which means the pods aren\'t being discovered by the service. From the previous steps, we checked the deployment and pod details. The deployment for reservation is running, and the pod is up, but the service isn\'t pointing to any endpoints.\n\nLooking at the service description, the selector is "io.kompose.service=reservation". The deployment\'s pod template also has the same label. So the labels match, which should allow the service to find the pods. But the endpoints are still empty. Maybe the issue is with the pod\'s IP or port?\n\nWait, the pod\'s container is running on port 8087, which matches the service\'s port. But maybe the pod isn\'t actually listening on that port? Or maybe there\'s a network policy blocking traffic? The logs don\'t show any errors, just startup messages. \n\nAnother angle: the service type is ClusterIP, which is the default. But if there\'s a problem with the cluster\'s network configuration, maybe the service isn\'t properly routing traffic. Or perhaps the pods aren\'t reachable due to some firewall rules or security policies. \n\nWait, the user mentioned that the service\'s endpoints are <none>. That usually means that the pods aren\'t being selected by the service. But the labels match. Maybe the pods are not in the same namespace? No, the service and deployment are in the hotel-reservation namespace. \n\nWait, maybe the pod\'s IP isn\'t being assigned correctly? Or the pod isn\'t ready yet? But the pod status shows READY as 1/1. Hmm. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is misconfigured. Let me check the service YAML again. The service\'s selector is io.kompose.service=reservation, which matches the deployment\'s pod labels. The port is 8087, which is the same as the container\'s port. \n\nWait, could there be a typo in the service\'s port or target port? Let me check the service\'s YAML. The service has port 8087 and targetPort 8087. That seems correct. \n\nWait, maybe the pods are not actually running? But the kubectl get pods shows reservation-7bb77b596b-8zbb6 is running. The logs also show the service registered in Consul. So the pod is up and running, and the service is registered. \n\nBut the endpoints are still empty. Maybe the service is not properly communicating with the kubelet? Or there\'s a problem with the kube-proxy? \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is not exposed correctly. Let me check the service\'s YAML again. The service is of type ClusterIP, which should work. \n\nWait, maybe the pods are not in the same namespace as the service? No, both are in hotel-reservation. \n\nHmm, this is tricky. The logs don\'t show any errors, and the pods are running. The service selector matches the pod labels. The only thing left is to check if the pods are actually reachable. Maybe a network policy is blocking the traffic. Or maybe the service is not properly configured to use the correct ports. \n\nWait, the service\'s port is 8087, and the pod\'s container is listening on 8087. So that should be okay. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is not being updated because the deployment is not rolling out correctly. But the deployment shows 1/1 replicas available. \n\nI\'m stuck. Maybe I should check the endpoints again. If the endpoints are still empty, maybe there\'s a problem with the kubelet or the API server. But that\'s more of a system-level issue. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is not properly configured to use the correct labels. Wait, the service\'s selector is io.kompose.service=reservation, and the pod\'s label is the same. So that should work. \n\nWait, maybe the service is not being created correctly. Let me check the service\'s YAML. The service\'s name is reservation, and the selector is correct. \n\nI think the issue might be that the service is not properly configured to use the correct ports or there\'s a network policy blocking the traffic. But without more information, it\'s hard to tell. The logs don\'t show any errors, so maybe the service is just not being discovered for some reason. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is not being exposed because the pods are not in the same namespace. But that\'s not the case here. \n\nI think the next step is to check the endpoints again to see if there\'s any information there. If the endpoints are still empty, maybe there\'s a deeper issue with the cluster configuration. But given the information we have, the most likely cause is that the service is not properly configured to select the pods, even though the labels match. Maybe there\'s a typo or a misconfiguration in the service\'s YAML. \n\nWait, the service\'s YAML might have a typo in the selector or the port. Let me check again. The service\'s selector is io.kompose.service=reservation, and the pod\'s label is the same. The port is 8087. \n\nI think the problem might be that the service is not properly configured, but I can\'t see any obvious issues. The logs don\'t show any errors, so maybe the service is just not being discovered for some reason. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is not being updated because the deployment is not rolling out correctly. But the deployment shows 1/1 replicas available. \n\nI\'m not sure. Given the information, the most likely root cause is that the service is not properly configured to select the pods, even though the labels match. But without more data, it\'s hard to confirm. The next step would be to check the service\'s YAML again for any possible typos or misconfigurations.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The logs show the `reservation` pod is running and registered with Consul, but the service still has no endpoints. This suggests a potential misconfiguration in the service\'s YAML or network policy blocking pod discovery. Checking the service\'s YAML will confirm if the selector, port, or other configurations are correct.  \n\ntool_call: {  \n  "command": "kubectl get service reservation -n hotel-reservation -o yaml"  \n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1326, 'prompt_tokens': 11576, 'total_tokens': 12902, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe3f-c201-76e0-a37e-4be0d611a862-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11576, 'output_tokens': 1326, 'total_tokens': 12902}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='529497c1-bcb8-426d-b09c-81d363cda1e8'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to figure out why the reservation service in their Kubernetes cluster isn't showing any endpoints. Earlier steps showed that the pod is running and registered with Consul, but the service hasn't detected it. The service's YAML might have the wrong selector or port settings.\n\nSo, the next step is to get the service's YAML configuration. That way, I can check if the selector matches the pod's labels. The service's selector should be io.kompose.service=reservation, which matches the pod's label. But maybe there's a typo or mismatch. Also, checking the ports to ensure the service is exposing the correct port (8087) that the pod is using. If the service is configured correctly, maybe there's a network policy blocking traffic, but since the pod is registered with Consul, that's less likely. Still, verifying the service YAML is crucial. Let's retrieve it and inspect the details.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get service reservation -n hotel-reservation -o yaml"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-85c50b0705c18621', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 235, 'prompt_tokens': 12581, 'total_tokens': 12816, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe40-04d7-7b91-a08b-a99bea120e3e-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get service reservation -n hotel-reservation -o yaml'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-85c50b0705c18621', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 12581, 'output_tokens': 235, 'total_tokens': 12816}), ToolMessage(content='apiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  annotations:\n    kompose.cmd: kompose convert\n    kompose.version: 1.22.0 (955b78124)\n    kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration: |\n      {"apiVersion":"v1","kind":"Service","metadata":{"annotations":{"kompose.cmd":"kompose convert","kompose.version":"1.22.0 (955b78124)"},"creationTimestamp":null,"labels":{"io.kompose.service":"reservation"},"name":"reservation","namespace":"hotel-reservation"},"spec":{"ports":[{"name":"8087","port":8087,"targetPort":8087}],"selector":{"io.kompose.service":"reservation"}},"status":{"loadBalancer":{}}}\n  creationTimestamp: "2026-03-17T23:57:08Z"\n  labels:\n    io.kompose.service: reservation\n  name: reservation\n  namespace: hotel-reservation\n  resourceVersion: "352222"\n  uid: 8e1bc892-56e3-4eeb-ab98-044a1361c43b\nspec:\n  clusterIP: 10.96.193.45\n  clusterIPs:\n  - 10.96.193.45\n  internalTrafficPolicy: Cluster\n  ipFamilies:\n  - IPv4\n  ipFamilyPolicy: SingleStack\n  ports:\n  - name: "8087"\n    port: 8087\n    protocol: TCP\n    targetPort: 8087\n  selector:\n    io.kompose.service: reservation\n  sessionAffinity: None\n  type: ClusterIP\nstatus:\n  loadBalancer: {}\n', id='1aed7cef-1a1f-4056-9862-5e8f86d02b6f', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-85c50b0705c18621'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='665b51e4-bfbb-45db-8072-6d172e51cbd6')]]
