exposed because the pods are not in the same namespace. But that\'s not the case here. \n\nI think the next step is to check the endpoints again to see if there\'s any information there. If the endpoints are still empty, maybe there\'s a deeper issue with the cluster configuration. But given the information we have, the most likely cause is that the service is not properly configured to select the pods, even though the labels match. Maybe there\'s a typo or a misconfiguration in the service\'s YAML. \n\nWait, the service\'s YAML might have a typo in the selector or the port. Let me check again. The service\'s selector is io.kompose.service=reservation, and the pod\'s label is the same. The port is 8087. \n\nI think the problem might be that the service is not properly configured, but I can\'t see any obvious issues. The logs don\'t show any errors, so maybe the service is just not being discovered for some reason. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is not being updated because the deployment is not rolling out correctly. But the deployment shows 1/1 replicas available. \n\nI\'m not sure. Given the information, the most likely root cause is that the service is not properly configured to select the pods, even though the labels match. But without more data, it\'s hard to confirm. The next step would be to check the service\'s YAML again for any possible typos or misconfigurations.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The logs show the `reservation` pod is running and registered with Consul, but the service still has no endpoints. This suggests a potential misconfiguration in the service\'s YAML or network policy blocking pod discovery. Checking the service\'s YAML will confirm if the selector, port, or other configurations are correct.  \n\ntool_call: {  \n  "command": "kubectl get service reservation -n hotel-reservation -o yaml"  \n}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata