 a problem. Exit code 2 usually indicates a problem with the application starting up.\n\nSo the next step is to check the pod\'s logs to see why the container exited with code 2. The user already tried getting the logs, but maybe they need to look deeper. Let me check the logs again. The logs might show an error related to the application not starting, like a missing dependency or a configuration issue. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is configured to use a different port than what the application is using. But according to the YAML, the service\'s port is 8087, and the container\'s port is also 8087. That should be correct. \n\nWait, the user also mentioned that the service registered with Consul. Maybe there\'s an issue with Consul integration. But the service\'s endpoints aren\'t showing up in Kubernetes, so maybe the problem is internal to Kubernetes\' service discovery. \n\nBut the main issue here is the container exiting with code 2. The logs should clarify why. Let me check the logs again using the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool to get the pod\'s logs. That should help identify the root cause of the container failure.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\nreasoning: The pod\'s container exited with code 2, indicating an application-level failure. Retrieving the pod\'s logs will reveal the specific error causing the container to terminate, which is critical for diagnosing the root cause of the service\'s inability to function.\n\ntool_call: {"name": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl -n hotel-reservation logs reservation-7bb77b596b-8zbb6"}}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 579, 'prompt_tokens': 4772, 'total_tokens': 5351, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run-