 But the logs show the containerPort is 8087, which matches the service\'s port. \n\nHmm. Maybe the issue is that the service is not properly communicating with the pods due to a network policy or a misconfigured Ingress. But without the right tools, it\'s hard to check. \n\nSince the user can\'t use \'get_network_policies\', maybe they should try to check the service\'s metrics again with a different query. For example, checking the number of active endpoints or the service\'s health. \n\nAlternatively, the user could try to restart the service or the pod. But how? They can\'t use \'kubectl rollout restart\' directly. \n\nWait, the user can use \'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\' to restart the deployment. Let me check. The service is part of a deployment called \'reservation\'. So they could try to rollout restart the deployment. \n\nBut first, they need to confirm the deployment name. The user previously checked the service and found that the deployment is \'reservation\'. So they can use \'kubectl rollout restart deployment reservation -n hotel-reservation\'. \n\nBut the user needs to make sure that the deployment exists. Let me check. The user previously checked the service and found that the deployment is healthy. So the deployment name is likely \'reservation\'. \n\nSo the next step would be to restart the deployment to see if that resolves the issue. \n\nAlternatively, the user could check the service\'s status again after a wait. \n\nBut given the available tools, the best next step is to restart the deployment using \'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\' with the appropriate command.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\nreasoning: The logs indicate the service registered successfully with Consul, but endpoints are missing. Restarting the deployment ensures any transient issues (e.g., container crashes, service discovery delays) are resolved. This action will redeploy the pod and re-register the service with Consul.\n\ntool_call: {"name": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely"